so today we're talking about commercial  architecture which means  no fancy tricks no transitions no  background changes  just straight to the point and very  commercial focused  what's going on guys my name is david  thomich and welcome back to the channel  today we're talking about my top five  tips for commercial architecture  and what you need to understand before  you go into a meeting with your first  commercial client  so commercial architecture is all about  business and understanding the business  itself  your new commercial client is going to  want a good understanding of  their business and you as a business  operator  so as an architect in commercial  practice it's all about you  understanding their business and their  business model  the better you understand this the more  you know about this  section of their life the more they're  going to want to use you as the sole  architect on their next commercial  project  so tip number two like i said no messing  around here today  is make sure you add value to their  business if you can understand their  business model you can definitely add  value to their business  for example if it's a restaurant it's  all about seating numbers and it's all  about  operational efficiency if you can get  people in and out of that restaurant  quicker or if you can get more tables in  a limited amount of space  you're doing these people a favor and in  the long term you're making them more  money  so you're definitely adding value to  their business and you're being a better  commercial architect  number three what you'll understand in  residential and in commercial  is budget blows out very quickly in  commercial  more so than residential because  commercial items cost  a fortune so if you're doing commercial  architecture make sure you brush up  on your knowledge on every single item  that might be going into this commercial  property  for example if you're doing a warehouse  they might be needing forklifts  racks sprinklers all sorts of different  things for  restaurants for example they might also  need some sort of commercial fridges  range hoods all sorts of different works  so it's your job to understand  these critical points in their operation  number four commercial is all about  timing  commercial projects are on a tight time  frame  more time equals more money for your  clients so make sure you get it done  in a timely manner and make sure you  establish this  very very early on no client wants you  to waste their time  and effectively waste their money in a  commercial project  especially if they've got a deadline to  open say it's  december january whatever 2022 2024  whenever it is you need to make that  deadline and you need to make sure  everybody in your project team is aware  of those deadlines  so remember time is very much so money  when it comes to commercial projects and  last but not least commercial  architecture is  very different to residential  architecture residential architecture is  more or less a simple process meet the  client  get it through council get attended get  it built it's  relatively simple when it comes  commercial there is so much more  involved so make sure  you understand the process and the  legalities behind it  the last thing you want to do is get  caught up towards the end of a project  because you didn't know  a step one of these steps could be the  make or break between an additional 30  days in this project that you completely  didn't even know about  so make sure you understand the  legislation in your country and  make sure that you understand the  processes of commercial architecture  anyway that's all for me today guys  extremely quick video  because it's more or less in line with  the commercial theme  time is money so make sure you listen to  these five tips and if you enjoyed the  video make sure you smash that like  button down below  if you want to see more of my content  smash that subscribe button because as  always  i'll 